\nL12: \nL13: Authors:cite6†Sureyya Akin , cite7†Kavita Srivastava , cite8†Prateek B. Kapoor , cite9†Pradeep G. Sethi , cite10†Sunita Q. Patel , cite11†Rahu Srivastava L14: \nL15: View a PDF of the paper titled From Pixels to Cooperation Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning based on Multimodal World Models, by Sureyya Akin and 4 other authors\nL16: \nL17: cite12†No PDF available, click to view other formats L18: > Abstract:Learning cooperative multi-agent policies directly from high-dimensional, multimodal sensory inputs like pixels and audio (from pixels) is notoriously sample-inefficient. Model-free Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) algorithms struggle with the joint challenge of representation learning, partial observability, and credit assignment. To address this, we propose a novel framework based on a shared, generative Multimodal World Model (MWM).\nL19: Our MWM is trained to learn a compressed latent representation of the environment's dynamics by fusing distributed, multimodal observations from all agents using a scalable attention-based mechanism. Subsequently, we leverage this learned MWM as a fast, \"imagined\" simulator to train cooperative MARL policies (e.g., MAPPO) entirely within its latent space, decoupling representation learning from policy learning.\nL20: We introduce a new set of challenging multimodal, multi-agent benchmarks built on a 3D physics simulator. Our experiments demonstrate that our MWM-MARL framework achieves orders-of-magnitude greater sample efficiency compared to state-of-the-art model-free MARL baselines.\nL21: We further show that our proposed multimodal fusion is essential for task success in environments with sensory asymmetry and that our architecture provides superior robustness to sensor-dropout, a critical feature for real-world deployment.\nL22: Comments:  | We have identified critical issues in the code implementation that severely deviate from Algorithm 1, invalidating all experimental results and conclusions. Despite exhaustive efforts to correct these issues, we find they fundamentally undermine the paper's core claims. To uphold academic integrity and prevent misinformation, we are withdrawing this manuscript\nL23: Subjects:  | Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\nL24: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.01310 [cs.MA]\nL25:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.01310v2 [cs.MA] for this version)\nL26:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01310†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Sureyya Akin [cite16†view email ]\nL30: cite5†[v1] Mon, 3 Nov 2025 07:44:56 UTC (1,419 KB)\nL31: [v2] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 01:34:30 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled From Pixels to Cooperation Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning based on Multimodal World Models, by Sureyya Akin and 4 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * Withdrawn\nL40: \nL41: No license for this version due to withdrawn\nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.MA\nL45: \nL46: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L47: \nL48: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite22†cs L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite26†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite27†What is Connected Papers?†www.con